do $do$
declare p jsonb := $pl$[{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-121-v2","t":"Proteins","s":"A structural protein in a marine animal consists of one polypeptide, and its compact folded form is required to provide support. Two oppositely charged R groups help stabilize this form through an ionic interaction. Researchers replace one of these amino acids so that the two R groups now have the same charge. The number of amino acids and all peptide-bond connections are retained. Which prediction best explains how this replacement could affect structural support?","a":"A","c":[["A","The changed R-group interaction may alter tertiary folding, making the protein less able to provide structural support.",true,"Replacing one amino acid changes the primary structure and replaces an attractive ionic interaction with repulsion between like charges. This can alter tertiary folding, weakening the compact shape required for structural support."],["B","Preserving the peptide-bond connections keeps the primary structure identical, so the protein must retain its shape and supporting function.",false,"Bond-integrity trap: Unbroken peptide bonds do not guarantee an unchanged amino acid sequence. The replacement changes primary structure and may therefore affect folding and support. Fix: Compare the original and modified amino acid identities before judging whether primary structure changed."],["C","The replacement directly changes secondary structure because ionic interactions between R groups define that level, potentially weakening structural support.",false,"Secondary–tertiary confusion: Secondary structure involves hydrogen bonding between backbone atoms, whereas the described ionic interaction involves R groups. Altering this interaction can affect tertiary structure and its supporting function. Fix: Locate the interacting atoms: backbone atoms or R groups."],["D","The replacement may weaken structural support by disrupting quaternary structure and causing the protein's polypeptide subunits to separate.",false,"Quaternary-structure trap: A protein with only one polypeptide has no quaternary structure to disrupt. Its supporting function can change when interactions within that single chain alter tertiary folding. Fix: Determine how many polypeptide chains are present before predicting subunit separation."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-121-v3","t":"Proteins","s":"An experimental adhesive contains a protein made of one polypeptide chain. The protein must retain a particular folded shape for the adhesive to hold surfaces together. A polar R group forms a hydrogen bond with another R group elsewhere in the chain. Researchers replace the first amino acid with one having a nonpolar R group, without changing the number of amino acids or their peptide-bond connectivity. Which prediction best explains a possible effect on adhesion?","a":"C","c":[["A","Unchanged peptide-bond connectivity means the primary structure is unchanged, so the protein must retain its adhesive function.",false,"Bond-integrity trap: Preserving peptide-bond connectivity does not preserve amino acid sequence when an amino acid is replaced. The changed primary structure can affect folding even though chain length stays constant. Fix: Check amino acid identities when deciding whether primary structure has changed."],["B","Loss of the R-group hydrogen bond directly changes secondary structure, because secondary structure is defined by hydrogen bonds between R groups.",false,"Secondary–tertiary confusion: Secondary structure involves hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, whereas the specified bond connects R groups. Disrupting this R-group interaction can affect tertiary structure and adhesive function. Fix: Locate the hydrogen-bond partners before assigning a structural level."],["C","The replacement may disrupt an R-group interaction, altering tertiary folding and weakening the protein’s adhesive function.",true,"The substituted R group can disrupt a hydrogen bond that helps maintain the protein’s three-dimensional shape. Because adhesion requires that shape, altered folding could weaken the adhesive."],["D","Loss of the R-group hydrogen bond separates polypeptide subunits, disrupting quaternary structure and weakening adhesion.",false,"Quaternary-structure trap: A protein with only one polypeptide has no separate polypeptide subunits to pull apart. Interactions between different regions of that chain help stabilize tertiary structure instead. Fix: Determine how many polypeptides are present before predicting subunit separation."]]}]$pl$::jsonb; r jsonb; c jsonb; v_ci uuid; v_civ uuid; n int;
begin
  if jsonb_array_length(p) <> 2 then raise exception 'chunk size'; end if;
  if exists (select 1 from app.content_items ci where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x))
    then raise exception 'chunk already loaded'; end if;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(p) loop
    insert into app.content_items (exam_pack_version_id, content_key, item_type, title, status)
    values ('2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7', r->>'k', 'mcq', r->>'t', 'draft') returning id into v_ci;
    insert into app.content_item_versions (content_item_id, version_num, stem, stimulus, content_hash, status, review_status, canonical_answer_1)
    values (v_ci, 1, r->>'s', null, md5(r->>'k'), 'draft', 'tutor_review_pending', r->>'a') returning id into v_civ;
    for c in select * from jsonb_array_elements(r->'c') loop
      insert into app.mcq_choices (content_item_version_id, choice_key, choice_text, is_correct, rationale)
      values (v_civ, c->>0, c->>1, (c->>2)::boolean, c->>3);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into n from app.content_items ci join app.content_item_versions v on v.content_item_id=ci.id join app.mcq_choices m on m.content_item_version_id=v.id
   where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.status='draft' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x);
  if n <> 8 then raise exception 'chunk 9: expected 8 choice rows, got %', n; end if;
  raise notice 'chunk 9: loaded 2 draft items';
end
$do$;
